"[2511.16233v1] FT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16233v1)\nciteturn2484view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16233v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Robotics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:FT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Kewei Chen , cite6†Yayu Long , cite7†Shuai Li , cite8†Mingsheng Shang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled FT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models, by Kewei Chen and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The powerful generalization of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models is bottlenecked by their heavy reliance on massive, redundant, and unevenly valued datasets, hindering their widespread application. Existing model-centric optimization paths, such as model compression (which often leads to performance degradation) or policy distillation (whose products are model-dependent and lack generality), fail to fundamentally address this data-level challenge.\nL17: To this end, this paper introduces FT-NCFM, a fundamentally different, data-centric generative data distillation framework. Our framework employs a self-contained Fact-Tracing (FT) engine that combines causal attribution with programmatic contrastive verification to assess the intrinsic value of samples. Guided by these assessments, an adversarial NCFM process synthesizes a model-agnostic, information-dense, and reusable data asset.\nL18: Experimental results on several mainstream VLA benchmarks show that models trained on just 5% of our distilled coreset achieve a success rate of 85-90% compared with training on the full dataset, while reducing training time by over 80%. Our work demonstrates that intelligent data distillation is a highly promising new path for building efficient, high-performance VLA models.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted at the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI-26)\nL20: Subjects:  | Robotics (cs.RO)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 68T40 (Primary) 68T05, 68T45 (Secondary)\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.9; I.2.6; I.2.10\nL23: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2511.16233 [cs.RO]\nL24:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2511.16233v1 [cs.RO] for this version)\nL25:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16233†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Kewei Chen [cite14†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 11:04:14 UTC (14,004 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled FT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models, by Kewei Chen and 3 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite9†View PDF L38:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite15†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite16†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.RO\nL45: \nL46: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L47: \nL48: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite22†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provi